"executive_summary": "Anomalous X-ray Pulsars (AXPs) are young isolated neutron stars powered by ultra-strong magnetic fields, and until 2004 they were known only as soft X-ray emitters. The discovery of a hard X-ray/soft gamma-ray component from several AXPs (1E 1841-045, 4U 0142+61, 1RXS J170849.0-400910) provided important insight but posed a serious challenge to explain its origin. The physical mechanism of the hard emission component has still not been fully resolved, motivating this work. This paper investigates the high-energy gamma-ray properties of the brightest AXP, 4U 0142+61, using data collected with the Large Area Telescope (LAT) on board Fermi Gamma-ray Space Telescope to establish the spectral behavior of the source on a very broad energy span and search for pulsed emission. The LAT operates in the energy band of 20 MeV-300 GeV and has been operating as an all sky monitor since 2008 August 4. The authors accumulated LAT data within a 15° radius centered at 4U 0142+61, collected from 2008 August 4 to 2010 April 29 with an exposure time of ~31.7 Ms, and performed unbinned likelihood analysis using ScienceTools v9r15p2 with P6 V3 DIFFUSE as the instrumental response. They also employed contemporaneous Rossi X-ray Timing Explorer (RXTE) observations to obtain the spin ephemeris of the source, selecting 53 RXTE observations performed between 2008 August 4 and 2010 April 30 with a total exposure time of about 196 ks. The search for persistent emission yielded no significant detection: the Test Statistics (TS) value is ~0.23 for the 15° region (detection significance less than 1σ) and ~3 for the 2° region (detection significance less than 4σ). The 3σ flux upper limits with a power law index 2.5 are 2.32 × 10^-6 MeV cm^-2 s^-1 in the 0.2-1.0 GeV band and 1.28 × 10^-6 MeV cm^-2 s^-1 in the 1.0-10.0 GeV band. For pulsed emission, both LAT pulse profiles are consistent with random fluctuations with respect to its mean, and the 3σ upper limit to the RMS pulsed fraction is 1.5% in the 0.2-1.0 GeV band and 2.3% in the 1.0-10.0 GeV band. The lower energy LAT passband (30-200 MeV) also resulted with no evidence of pulsed emission; the 3σ RMS pulsed fraction upper limit is 1.6%. The authors constructed the νFν spectrum of the source in the 15 keV-10 GeV range by adopting the hard X-ray spectrum presented in den Hartog et al. (2008) and placing the upper limits calculated in this work. The upper limit curve is a power law with an index of -0.76, and these two curves intersect with each other at ~1.1 MeV, which is an upper limit to the spectral break energy. This estimate is in accordance with the coronal emission model by Beloborodov & Thompson (2007) and the quantum electrodynamics model by Heyl & Hernquist (2005a,b). The main conclusion is that no significant high-energy gamma-ray emission is detected, but the obtained upper limits help constrain existing physical models. The results are important because our LAT upper limits are much lower than high pulsed fraction (up to 100%) seen in hard X-rays with INTEGRAL (den Hartog et al. 2008).",
